/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  relief02_acetone_fire_case -- THE FLUID SIDE OF A FIRE-CASE RELIEF VALVE
  (DEV.md 4c C61, the "Designing for overpressure" EduTool).

  THE QUESTION.  A drum of acetone, a flammable solvent, sits in a pool
  fire.  Its relief valve opens at the set pressure and the pressure climbs
  to the relieving pressure P1 = set + allowed accumulation; there the
  liquid BOILS, and the
  fire's heat leaves as vapour.  The relief load is the heat absorbed
  divided by the latent heat, and the valve passes that vapour.  So four
  properties of the fluid decide the valve, and all four are computed here:

      T_bubble       the boiling temperature at P1 (the relieving temperature)
      Hvap_acetone   the latent heat of vaporisation at that temperature
      Z              the vapour's compressibility factor at (T, P1)
      gamma          Cp/Cv of the vapour (the ideal-gas ratio, Cp_ig/(Cp_ig-R))

  THE NUMBERS ARE AUTHOR-SET, and the EduTool moves them.  P is the
  relieving pressure of a drum whose MAWP is 5 barg, protected for the fire
  case with the set pressure at the MAWP: 1.21 x 5 barg + 1.01325 bar
  = 7.06325 bar absolute.  The 5 barg is a round number chosen for the
  lesson, not a property of any real drum.  T is the boiling temperature
  the SAME run reports at that pressure (T_bubble), written back by hand so
  that Z, gamma and the latent heat are evaluated AT the relieving state;
  the EduTool does the same thing live, in two runs, whenever the reader
  moves the MAWP, and its test holds this file's T to its own T_bubble.

  WHERE EACH NUMBER COMES FROM.  T_bubble: the bubble point of pure acetone
  on the package in constant/thermoPhysPropDict (ideal liquid, SRK vapour).
  Hvap_acetone: the record's HvapTb carried to T by the Watson correlation,
  Component::Hvap_latent -- the same term the engine subtracts from the
  ideal-gas enthalpy to price every liquid stream.  Z: the SRK vapour root.
  gamma: the record's ideal-gas heat capacity.

  WHY ACETONE AND NOT TOLUENE.  The first draft of this case was a toluene
  drum, and its run announced that toluene's Antoine record is fitted only
  from 273 to 380 K -- below the normal boiling point, so every boiling
  temperature of a pressurised toluene drum is an EXTRAPOLATED vapour
  pressure.  A relief temperature resting on an extrapolation is a weak
  first lesson.  Acetone's record covers 259.16 to 507.60 K, which holds the
  relieving state of any drum this EduTool lets the reader set.

  WHAT THIS CASE DOES NOT SAY.  Nothing here is a relief-valve design: the
  sizing is the EduTool's arithmetic on these numbers, and no number in
  either is a design for a real vessel.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
operations
(
    {
        name        relieving;
        type        propertyPoint;
        state
        {
            T            394.109436 K;
            P            706325 Pa;
            composition  { acetone 1.0; }
        }
        properties  ( T_bubble Hvap_acetone );
    }
);
